```python
def rotate_90_clockwise(mat):
    n = len(mat)
    # transpose
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(i + 1, n):
            mat[i][j], mat[j][i] = mat[j][i], mat[i][j]
    # reverse each row
    for row in mat:
        row.reverse()
```

Both passes touch each element a constant number of times, so the whole rotation is O(n²) time for an n×n matrix — proportional to the number of elements, which is the best possible since every element must be examined at least once. No second matrix is allocated, so it's O(1) extra space.
